# ARCHITECTURE.md

# Palisade Architecture

## Overview

Palisade is a Godot 4 C# first-person dungeon/maze game. It uses a 2D piece-based map editor to define dungeon layouts, then generates a playable 3D dungeon from those layouts.

The game is built around modular maze pieces that must connect naturally through openings. Some pieces are flat rooms/corridors, while others are stairs that connect multiple levels.

## Tech Stack

- Engine: Godot 4.x
- Language: C#
- Project file: `project.godot`
- Runtime scripts: `scripts/*.cs`
- Scenes: `scenes/*.tscn`

## Core Flow

1. The user places pieces in the 2D map editor.
2. Each piece stores type/value, position, and rotation.
3. Piece openings are checked so neighboring pieces connect legally.
4. Map data is saved/loaded through serializer logic.
5. On game start or generation, the dungeon builder reads the map layout.
6. The dungeon builder creates 3D floors, walls, ramps, collisions, and center arena connections.
7. The player spawns into the generated dungeon.

## System Responsibilities

### Map Editor

Main files:

- `scripts/MapEditorMain.cs`
- `scripts/TileData.cs`
- `scripts/MapSerializer.cs`

Responsibilities:

- piece selection
- piece placement
- piece rotation
- layout editing
- layout save/load
- preparing data for generation

### Piece Database

Main file:

- `scripts/PieceDB.cs`

Responsibilities:

- defining piece types
- returning openings
- handling rotation
- stair up/down direction
- determining connection metadata

Important risk:
If piece openings or stair directions are wrong here, validation and 3D generation will both behave incorrectly.

### Map Validation

Main file:

- `scripts/MapValidator.cs`

Responsibilities:

- checking whether adjacent pieces connect correctly
- rejecting impossible layouts
- protecting generator from invalid map data

### Dungeon Builder

Main file:

- `scripts/DungeonBuilder.cs`

Responsibilities:

- converting tile layout into 3D geometry
- creating floors, walls, ramps, and collisions
- handling stair geometry
- filling blocked walls
- leaving valid openings clear
- stitching maps to the center arena / middle room

Important risk:
Most visible generation bugs probably live here, especially missing walls, wrong openings, bad collisions, and arena stitching.

### Multi-cell Pieces (Room, Pit)

Main files:

- `scripts/PieceDB.cs` (`Footprint`, `FloorSpan`, `IsMultiCell`)
- `scripts/RoomGroups.cs`
- `scripts/RoomBuilder.cs`
- `scripts/PitBuilder.cs`

Most pieces occupy exactly one `(X, Y, Floor)` cell. `Room` and `Pit` are
built differently, and any future piece that also spans more than one cell
should follow the same contract rather than inventing its own:

- **`Room`** is stored as one independent `MazePiece` per cell — painting a
  rectangle in the editor writes N separate single-cell `Room` tiles, not one
  rectangle object. `Room` declares openings on all four faces at every
  rotation, so every existing per-tile system (validator BFS, serializer,
  gold cost, single-cell deletion) already handles it correctly with no
  changes. `RoomGroups.Build` flood-fills adjacent `Room` tiles into
  connected `RoomGroup`s at build/render time only — **`RoomGroups` is the
  seam between per-tile storage and per-space geometry.** Nothing upstream of
  it (editor, validator, serializer) ever sees a "room" as more than a
  scatter of 1x1 tiles; `RoomBuilder.Build` is the only place that turns a
  `RoomGroup` back into one merged floor, perimeter-only walls, and one
  continuous vault.
- **`Pit`** is a genuinely multi-cell piece: one `MazePiece` at the shaft's
  centre (its anchor) represents a 3x3 footprint spanning up to 3 floors.
  `PieceDB.Footprint(t)` returns the cell offsets from the anchor and
  `PieceDB.FloorSpan(t)` returns the floor offsets, both clipped to the
  world's floor range at the point of use; `PieceDB.IsMultiCell(t)` is true
  only for `Pit` today.

**`PieceDB.Footprint` / `PieceDB.FloorSpan` are the only sanctioned way to ask
what volume a piece claims.** Editor occupancy
(`MazeEditor3D.TryGetPlacedPieceOnFloor`), placement validation
(`MazeEditor3D.IsValidPlacement`), and validator connectivity
(`MazeValidator.IsGameReady`'s BFS) all resolve a piece's volume through these
two methods rather than hard-coding 3x3/3-floor math of their own. A future
multi-cell piece type that implements `Footprint` and `FloorSpan` inherits
editor occupancy and BFS connectivity for free — no other system needs to
change. Both methods return a **fresh copy** of their backing array on every
call; do not change them to return a shared reference — an earlier version did
exactly that, and one caller mutating the result in place corrupted every
other piece type's footprint for the rest of the process's life.

Geometry for both piece types is emitted **per (cell, floor)**, one
`StaticBody3D`/mesh set per cell (matching `DungeonBuilder.BuildFloor`'s
convention), never one mesh merged across a whole room group or shaft — this
renderer's GL-compatibility backend lights a mesh from its bounding box centre,
so a large merged mesh leaves its corners dark. Continuity across cell borders
(the room vault, the pit's dome) is preserved by always computing the
arch/vault height from the *group's* or *shaft's* world-space bounds, never a
per-cell offset, so adjacent cells agree on height at their shared edge with
no seam.

Important risk: any decoration pass that keys off a piece's stored
`(X, Y, Floor)` — e.g. `PieceVariants`, which looks up a body by
`Cell_{p.X}_{p.Y}_{p.Floor}` — must explicitly account for `IsMultiCell`
pieces, since the anchor cell does not necessarily have real floor geometry
under it (a `Pit`'s anchor floor is usually its open middle level). Any
editor action that can implicitly delete a piece (painting over it, dropping
another piece onto it) must check for an occupied multi-cell footprint first
and refuse, the same way `Start`/`Exit` are protected — this editor has no
undo.

